 <div class="ghead"><span class="k">Section 11</span><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2></div>
 <div class="prose"><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is GWO certification?</summary><div class="a">GWO (Global Wind Organisation) is the safety training standard set jointly by turbine manufacturers and wind farm owners. A GWO certificate is the common entry document, valid on the site of any company that recognises the standard.</div></details><details><summary>How many days is GWO BST?</summary><div class="a">Four days. Working at heights and rescue 2 days, first aid 1 day, fire awareness half a day, manual handling half a day. Adding Sea Survival for offshore takes it to five.</div></details><details><summary>How long is a GWO certificate valid?</summary><div class="a">Two years. You take the refresher (BST Refresher) before it expires and validity extends by another two years. Once it has expired you cannot refresh; you retake the full four-day BST.</div></details><details><summary>Who pays for GWO training?</summary><div class="a">As a rule the employer: the BST and refresher of a technician in employment are the employer&#8217;s responsibility. What happens in practice is different &#8212; most people entering the industry pay for it themselves out of fear of not finding work, and it does work, because companies favour a candidate who can be on site immediately. If you are not in a hurry, go to interviews first and ask who covers training and refresher; if you are paying yourself, take BST only, verify the centre, and use that certificate as leverage in the interview.</div></details><details><summary>How much does GWO training cost?</summary><div class="a">It varies by centre, by season and by exchange rate, so I do not give a fixed figure here; most fixed prices you find online come from pages that are out of date. Ask three accredited centres for a quote covering all four modules, WINDA registration and equipment.</div></details><details><summary>How do I know a training centre is GWO accredited?</summary><div class="a">Check GWO&#8217;s own provider search. The list shows the company name, the city of the training site and which modules it is certified for. The certain measure is your certificate appearing in the WINDA record after the course.</div></details><details><summary>What is WINDA?</summary><div class="a">GWO&#8217;s training record system. Every module you take is entered against your WINDA ID and employers verify validity there. The record belongs to you, not the company; it comes with you when you change jobs.</div></details><details><summary>Should I take BTT?</summary><div class="a">Not mandatory, but it sets your CV apart. If you are starting out, do not take it immediately: get on site first, see whether you lean mechanical or electrical, and take that side&#8217;s module in your second year.</div></details><details><summary>Is GWO the same as statutory safety training?</summary><div class="a">No. Occupational health and safety training required by law (in Turkey, under law no. 6331) is a legal obligation; GWO is an industry standard. Both are asked for separately when you enter a site and neither replaces the other.</div></details></div></div>
 </section>
