 <div class="ghead"><span class="k">Section 10</span><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2></div>
 <div class="prose"><div class="faq"><details><summary>What should I study to become a wind turbine technician?</summary><div class="a">There is no single required subject. On site you will mostly find graduates of electrical, electromechanical, mechanical, mechatronics and automotive technology. A two-year degree is enough; a full degree is not required. What decides it is not the subject but your GWO certificates, technical English and hand skills.</div></details><details><summary>I have no experience at all. Can I start directly?</summary><div class="a">Yes. Installation crews hire without experience, and it is where you learn the site fastest. Getting into a service team without experience is harder. Be in a position to apply with a medical certificate and GWO BST.</div></details><details><summary>Can I get hired without a GWO certificate?</summary><div class="a">You can be hired, but you cannot go on site. Some companies cover the GWO training of the person they hire and arrange it in the first two weeks; so if you are not in a hurry, go to the interviews first and ask. On the other hand a candidate with the certificate ready often moves ahead, because they can be on site within days. Both are true; the "who pays" section on the GWO page will help you decide which applies to you.</div></details><details><summary>Is there an age limit for starting?</summary><div class="a">There is no legal upper limit; what decides it is the medical certificate. I have seen people start at 19 and someone climb a tower for the first time at 45. If you are starting over 35, it makes more sense to aim at service or commissioning rather than erection.</div></details><details><summary>Do women work as technicians on site?</summary><div class="a">Yes, and the numbers are growing. The physical requirements are the same for everyone: the medical certificate, rescue capability and the equipment weight limits. The technique taught in the manual handling module is built on doing the work with correct movement, not with force.</div></details><details><summary>Is English essential?</summary><div class="a">At reading level, yes. Service manuals, fault code descriptions and manufacturer bulletins are in English. Speaking is only needed on projects where you work with an international crew.</div></details><details><summary>Is it possible to work abroad?</summary><div class="a">It is possible, and the biggest earnings in the industry are there. On European installation projects the daily per diem changes the picture completely. Your passport needs to be ready and any national service obligations completed; offers usually require you to travel at short notice.</div></details><details><summary>How much will I earn?</summary><div class="a">Most of the figures you find online are copied from one another and out of date. Six levers set the pay: manufacturer or contractor, working both trades, site allowance and shift, overseas per diem, certification level and experience. Use the offer reader on the pay page to read an offer properly.</div></details></div></div>
 </section>
